-- title:
--   Fontaine's point criterion passes to extensions of group schemes
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring and $p$ a prime that is a non-zero-divisor in $\mathcal O$, and let $k$ be a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\mathcal O \to k$ surjective and kernel the ideal $(p)$. Let $\mathcal R$ be a Hopf algebra and $\mathcal R_1, \mathcal R_3$ bialgebras over $\mathcal O$, let $\pi\colon \mathcal R \to \mathcal R_1$ be a surjective bialgebra map and $j\colon \mathcal R_3 \to \mathcal R$ a bialgebra map such that $\ker \pi$ is the ideal generated by $j(\ker(\text{counit of }\mathcal R_3))$ and such that $j$ is faithfully flat and finite as a ring map. For a commutative ring $A$ that is a $k$-bialgebra, [`Deformation.wittHom k p n A`](def/Dieudonne_WittVectorHom.html#L246) denotes the additive subgroup of truncated Witt vectors of length $n$ over $A$ that are primitive (their image under the comultiplication equals the sum of the images under the two inclusions $A \to A \otimes_k A$); [`Deformation.TruncWitt.fontaineKer p n π`](def/Dieudonne_FontaineHodge.html#L76) for a ring map $\pi\colon \mathcal S \to A$ consists of those truncated Witt vectors that are the length-$n$ truncation of $\pi_*X$ for some Witt vector $X$ over $\mathcal S$ whose $(n-1)$st ghost component lies in $(p^n)$; and [`Deformation.fontaineHodge k p π`](def/Dieudonne_FontaineHodge.html#L272) is the subgroup of the Dieudonné module $\varinjlim_n \mathrm{wittHom}\,k\,p\,n\,A$ generated by the classes of primitive vectors lying in these kernels, taken here for $\pi$ the right inclusion $\mathcal R_i \to k \otimes_{\mathcal O} \mathcal R_i$. Say that a $k$-algebra map $\psi\colon k \otimes_{\mathcal O} \mathcal S \to k \otimes_{\mathcal O} Y$ satisfies Fontaine's condition if for all $n$ it carries primitive truncated Witt vectors of length $n$ over $k \otimes_{\mathcal O}\mathcal S$ lying in `fontaineKer` for the inclusion of $\mathcal S$ into `fontaineKer` for the inclusion of $Y$. Assume: the map on Dieudonné modules induced by $\mathrm{id}_k \otimes \pi$ sends `fontaineHodge` of $\mathcal R$ onto `fontaineHodge` of $\mathcal R_1$; for every finite flat $\mathcal O$-algebra $Y'$, every $k$-algebra map $k \otimes_{\mathcal O} \mathcal R_1 \to k \otimes_{\mathcal O} Y'$ satisfying Fontaine's condition is $\mathrm{id}_k \otimes x$ for some $\mathcal O$-algebra map $x\colon \mathcal R_1 \to Y'$; and for every such $Y'$ the base-change map on $\mathcal O$-algebra maps $\mathcal R \to Y'$ is injective. Let finally $Y$ be a finite flat $\mathcal O$-algebra for which the same descent property holds for $\mathcal R_3$, and let $\chi\colon k \otimes_{\mathcal O} \mathcal R \to k \otimes_{\mathcal O} Y$ be a $k$-algebra map satisfying Fontaine's condition. Then $\chi = \mathrm{id}_k \otimes x$ for some $\mathcal O$-algebra map $x\colon \mathcal R \to Y$.
--
--   This is the dévissage step in Fontaine's description of the $Y$-points of a finite flat group scheme over $\mathcal O$ as those points of the special fibre that satisfy the condition cut out by his submodule of the Dieudonné module: the criterion is transferred from a quotient $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal R_1$ and a sub-object $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal R_3$ to the extension $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal R$, given right-exactness of the Fontaine submodule along $\pi$ and injectivity of reduction on points. It is used by [`Deformation.exists_algHom_baseChange_eq_of_forall_map_mem_fontaineKer_of_forall_ker_eq_torsionIdeal_zmodp`](thm.html#Deformation.exists_algHom_baseChange_eq_of_forall_map_mem_fontaineKer_of_forall_ker_eq_torsionIdeal_zmodp), where the criterion is established for the group schemes relevant to the deformation theory.
